export const blockPrompts: Record<string, string> = {
  "particle-field-hero": "# Particle Field hero\n\nBuild an original React hero called Particle Field. Do not imitate any existing motion library, template, or named section. Invent the layout from this brief.\n\n## Stack\n\n- React client component, Tailwind utility classes\n- A 2D canvas for the field. No WebGL, no image assets\n- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`\n\n## Scene\n\nFull-viewport ink background (`#07080c`). Behind the canvas, paint a static fallback: two soft radial washes (brass at the upper left, cool gray-blue at the lower right) and five small dots. If the canvas context is missing, that fallback is the whole effect.\n\nOn the canvas, draw about 110 points. Each point drifts slowly and bounces inside the parent. When two points are closer than roughly 120 pixels, stroke a line whose opacity fades with distance. Lines are brass (`rgba(214, 168, 96, …)`), points are warm paper white. A pointer inside the section gently pushes nearby points away. On a small preview, use about 40 points and a shorter link distance.\n\nPause the animation loop when `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` is set, but still paint one frame so the field is visible. Recompute the canvas size with a `ResizeObserver` and cap device pixel ratio around 1.75.\n\n## Type\n\nBottom or vertically centered, left aligned:\n\n- Eyebrow: `ATLAS FIELD`\n- Headline, serif: `Quiet sky, loud signal.`\n- One sentence of body copy about distance and a single invitation\n- Two buttons: `Enter the field` (paper fill) and `Read the notes` (ghost)\n\nUse `font-family: var(--font-serif), Georgia, serif` for the headline. Decorative canvas is `aria-hidden`. The section has an accessible name.\n\n## Done when\n\nThe field moves on its own, reacts slightly to the pointer, freezes under reduced motion, and still reads as a composed hero if canvas never starts.\n",
  "glass-refraction-hero": "# Glass refraction hero\n\nBuild an original React hero called Glass Refraction. Do not copy a known glassmorphism template. The point is a smoked panel sitting in front of a word that has been optically bent.\n\n## Stack\n\n- React client component and Tailwind\n- One inline SVG filter (`feTurbulence` + `feDisplacementMap`)\n- CSS keyframes only. No WebGL\n\n## Scene\n\nViewport background `#0c0b10`. Two large blurred orbs drift slowly: a brass orb and a muted sea-glass orb. Give each its own 18–22s ease-in-out loop.\n\nBehind the panel, set a huge serif word, `Halcyon`, in very low-contrast white. Apply the displacement filter to that word only. Use `useId` so the filter id is unique. Under reduced motion, set the displacement scale to 0 and stop the orb loops.\n\nCenter a real glass card:\n\n- `rounded-[28px]`, white border at about 25% opacity, `bg-white/10`, `backdrop-blur-xl`\n- A sheen pseudo-layer (a translucent vertical band) translates across the card on a 5.5s loop\n- Eyebrow `LUMEN ATELIER`\n- Serif headline: `Glass that keeps a little of the light.`\n- Short body copy for a fictional instrument house\n- Buttons: `View the glass` and `Book a sitting`\n\nStop the sheen and orb animations when `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`.\n\n## Done when\n\nThe card is readable, the word behind it looks slightly warped, the sheen crosses and returns, and reduced motion leaves a still, sharp composition.\n",
  "orbit-objects-hero": "# Orbit objects hero\n\nBuild an original hero called Orbit Objects: a dark studio with three floating forms. Do not reproduce a stock 3D landing template.\n\n## Stack\n\n- Next.js client components\n- `three`, `@react-three/fiber`, `@react-three/drei`\n- Load the canvas with `next/dynamic` and `ssr: false`\n- Wrap it in a React error boundary that renders nothing on failure\n\n## Always-on fallback\n\nThe section background is `#0c0d11` with a faint central glow. Before WebGL, and whenever WebGL is skipped, show three CSS shapes:\n\n- A pewter sphere (radial gradient, slow spin)\n- A copper triangle via `clip-path`\n- A small sea-glass bead\n\nThese shapes stay in the DOM under the canvas.\n\n## WebGL scene\n\nSkip the canvas when `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` is set, and also when a `preview` prop is true (gallery thumbnails must not start a context).\n\nWhen it mounts:\n\n- Camera around `[0, 0.15, 5.1]`, fov 40, transparent canvas\n- Warm key light and a cool rim light\n- A silver torus knot, a copper octahedron, and a pale sphere\n- `Float` from drei plus a slow `useFrame` rotation\n- Cap pixel ratio at 1.6\n\n## Type\n\nAnchored to the bottom over a fade:\n\n- Eyebrow `KEPLER ROOM`\n- Serif headline `Objects, holding still in motion.`\n- One sentence explaining that the still shapes remain if the scene cannot start\n\n## Done when\n\nThe page paints the CSS studio immediately, the 3D scene replaces it only after the chunk loads, and reduced motion never requests the WebGL module.\n",
  "horizon-veil": "# Horizon veil background\n\nBuild an original full-bleed background called Horizon Veil. It is a night field with one hot horizon and two thin ribbons. Do not recreate a generic aurora blob.\n\n## Stack\n\n- React client component for the shell\n- A separate module that mounts an OGL full-screen triangle\n- The GL module is `import()`ed only after the section intersects the viewport\n- Children are optional; render a default caption if none are passed\n\n## Static fallback, always present\n\nPaint this in CSS so the section looks finished before the shader, and forever if the shader never starts:\n\n- Base `#07090e`\n- A blurred copper ellipse sitting low, gently rising and falling\n- A 1px cream horizon line\n- Faint vertical streaks masked so they exist only above the horizon\n\nStop the CSS motion under `prefers-reduced-motion`.\n\n## Shader\n\nLazy-load it. Do not load it in preview mode or when reduced motion is requested.\n\nFragment shader behavior:\n\n- Dark top, slightly warmer mid\n- A horizon near 33% height that breathes with a slow sine\n- A cream core and a copper falloff\n- Two ribbons above the horizon, one tide-colored and one copper, each with its own phase\n- Very faint vertical veils\n- Fine grain and a vignette\n\nResize with the parent. On cleanup, cancel the frame and release the WebGL context. If the import throws, hide the canvas and leave the CSS.\n\n## Default caption\n\nEyebrow `HORIZON VEIL`. Serif headline `Night, and a line of heat.`\n\n## Done when\n\nThe CSS horizon is visible on first paint, the shader covers it only after scroll-into-view, and a reduced-motion visit never downloads the GL module.\n",
  "ember-mesh": "# Ember mesh background\n\nBuild an original CSS background called Ember Mesh. No canvas, no video, no images.\n\n## Scene\n\nCoal field `#100c0a`. Three blurred radial orbs move on long, independent loops:\n\n- Hot orange, upper left\n- Pale gold, mid right\n- Deep rust, lower left\n\nOver that, a 72px grid of hairline lines, masked with a radial gradient so the grid fades before the edges.\n\nStop every animation under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`. The orbs simply sit where they started. That still state is the fallback and the reduced-motion design. It should still look composed.\n\n## Content\n\nAccept `children`. If none are passed, show:\n\n- Eyebrow `EMBER MESH`\n- Serif headline `A pulse under the coal.`\n\nUse `var(--font-serif), Georgia, serif`.\n\n## Done when\n\nThe background loops slowly, never depends on JavaScript to be visible, and becomes a still image under reduced motion.\n",
  "scroll-text-reveal": "# Scroll text reveal\n\nBuild an original section that lights a sentence word by word as it scrolls. Do not use a pinned horizontal scroll or a letter-scramble effect.\n\n## Copy\n\nSplit this sentence on spaces and render each word as an inline-block:\n\n\"We cut the night into frames, then give each frame a place to breathe, a color to keep, and a name you can find in the dark.\"\n\nEyebrow: `MANIFEST`. Type is large serif, paper on `#090909`. The section is about 150vh so there is room to scrub.\n\n## Motion\n\nWords start fully visible (progressive enhancement). After GSAP and ScrollTrigger load, set them to opacity 0.16 and tween them to 1 with `scrub: true` while the section moves through the viewport. Stagger the words. Clean up the GSAP context on unmount, including when the import resolves after the component has gone.\n\nIf `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, do not import GSAP. Leave every word at full opacity.\n\n## Preview mode\n\nWhen a `preview` prop is true, do not attach ScrollTrigger. Instead, cycle word opacity with a CSS keyframe and a small per-word delay so a short card still feels alive. Reduced motion disables that animation too.\n\n## Done when\n\nScrolling brightens the sentence in order, a no-JS view shows the full sentence, and reduced motion never hides the words.\n",
  "bento-signal": "# Bento signal\n\nBuild an original feature bento for a fictional stills archive called Reelhouse. Five ideas, not a generic SaaS feature list.\n\n## Cells\n\n1. `Index every frame` — wide cell with an SVG sparkline. The stroke is drawn once with `stroke-dashoffset`, brass `#e7b267`.\n2. `Color notes` — three swatches: brass, sea glass, paper.\n3. `48k stills` — a large serif figure.\n4. `Quiet search` — a pill that reads `sodium coast` with a static caret.\n5. `Shared reels` — four overlapping initial circles: A, M, S, J.\n\nUse a 4-column grid on desktop. The first cell spans two columns and two rows. Cards are `rounded-2xl`, hairline white borders, `bg-white/[0.03]`, page background `#0d0d0f`.\n\n## Motion\n\nWith the `motion` package, fade and rise each cell when it enters the viewport, once, with a short stagger. Under `prefers-reduced-motion`, skip the offset and show the cells immediately. The chart stroke should also finish instantly under reduced motion.\n\nHeading: eyebrow `REELHOUSE`, serif title `The desk that keeps the frames.`\n\n## Done when\n\nThe grid reads as one instrument panel, the line draws in, and reduced motion shows the finished grid without waiting.\n",
  "spotlight-follow": "# Spotlight follow\n\nBuild an original section where a warm pool of light tracks the pointer across four chapters. No canvas.\n\n## Chapters\n\n1. Arrival — The room is already dim when you step in.\n2. The long cut — One take, held until the grain starts to speak.\n3. Color quiet — Sodium, ink, and a pale window left open.\n4. Credits — Names arrive like lights along a platform.\n\nDark stage `#090909`. Cards are translucent with a hairline border. Eyebrow `CHAPTERS`. Serif headline `Where the light lands.`\n\n## Light\n\nRegister two custom properties, `--sx` and `--sy`, with `@property` so they can interpolate. A full-bleed overlay uses:\n\n`radial-gradient(380px circle at var(--sx) var(--sy), rgba(244, 228, 196, 0.22), transparent 62%)`\n\nOn pointer move, set the properties from the pointer position inside the section. Do not store the position in React state.\n\nIn a `preview` prop mode, animate `--sx` and `--sy` on an 8s loop so the thumbnail moves on its own.\n\nIf `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, do not follow the pointer and do not run the loop. Leave the pool parked near the upper left.\n\n## Done when\n\nMoving across the section shifts the light, cards stay readable, and reduced motion is a still spotlight.\n",
  "logo-marquee": "# Logo marquee\n\nBuild an original infinite ribbon of fictional studios. Do not use real company names or logos.\n\n## Marks\n\nNorthline, Kelp & Co, Vellum, Arcadia Lab, Sable, Juniper, Halden, Mira Form.\n\nEach item is a circular monogram (first letter, brass hairline) plus the name in tracked uppercase. Invent only these names.\n\n## Motion\n\nDuplicate the list and translate the track from `0` to `-50%` on a 32s linear loop. Pause the animation while the ribbon is hovered. Edge-to-edge, with a hairline rule above and below.\n\nSection title, above the ribbon: eyebrow `ON THE REEL`, serif headline `Studios that keep the lights low.` Background `#090909`.\n\n## Reduced motion\n\nIf `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, do not duplicate the list and do not animate. Lay the eight studios out in a wrapping row. That static row is the fallback.\n\n## Done when\n\nThe ribbon loops seamlessly, pauses on hover, and becomes a still wrap under reduced motion.\n",
  "pricing-rise": "# Pricing rise\n\nBuild an original three-seat price section for a fictional screening room. No toggle, no comparison table.\n\n## Plans\n\n- Aisle — $0 — A single screening. Points: One seat, Evening program, Printed notes.\n- Balcony — $18 — The seat we keep lit. Points: Reserved row, Color stills, Quiet bar. This card is featured.\n- Gallery — $42 — A room of your own. Points: Private hour, Projectionist, After-notes.\n\nBackground `#0c0b0a`. Cards have hairline borders. The featured card is wrapped in a rotating conic-gradient rim (brass wedge on transparent) clipped by `overflow-hidden` and 1px of padding. The inner panel is `#16130f`.\n\n## Motion\n\nUse `motion` to rise each card into view once. Count the price from 0 to its target with `animate` when the card is in view, writing the number into a span so React state is not involved. Prefix a small `$`.\n\nUnder `prefers-reduced-motion`, show the final prices immediately, skip the rise, and do not spin the rim. A static brass edge is enough; you can leave the gradient without rotation.\n\nEyebrow `SEATS`. Serif headline `Choose how close you sit.` Each card ends with a button `Take {name}`.\n\n## Done when\n\nThe middle card is clearly the one that is lit, numbers arrive once, and reduced motion is calm and complete.\n",
  "beam-cta": "# Beam call to action\n\nBuild an original closing band. Hairline beams travel across a dark field. No particles, no 3D.\n\n## Scene\n\nBackground `#080808`. An absolutely positioned SVG, `preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"`, holds three diagonal lines:\n\n- Brass\n- Paper white at lower opacity\n- Sea glass\n\nEach line uses a dashed stroke and animates `stroke-dashoffset` at a slightly different duration so they do not march together. Add a soft brass radial glow behind the type.\n\n## Copy\n\nCentered on large screens, left on a short preview:\n\n- Eyebrow `LAST LIGHT`\n- Serif headline `Leave a light on for the next reel.`\n- One sentence that says the beams are decoration and the invitation is the thing that needs a click\n- Button `Start a screening`\n\n## Reduced motion\n\nStop the dash animation. The lines remain drawn. The glow and the type do not move.\n\n## Done when\n\nThe beams feel like light traveling, the headline is the focus, and reduced motion leaves a still poster.\n",
  "launch-reel": "# Launch reel\n\nBuild an original full landing page for a fictional night train, Vesper Line. It is one self-contained component, not a collage of other blocks. Do not borrow layout from a known product page.\n\n## Opening card\n\nFull viewport, background `#0b0c10`, a faint brass glow at the top.\n\n- Top row: `VESPER LINE` and `23:41`, tracked uppercase\n- Eyebrow `NIGHT SERVICE`\n- Enormous serif title, `Vesper` and an italic `Line`\n- Bottom route: `Harbor → Glass City`\n- A soft horizontal scan, a translucent band, travels down the card on a 7s loop\n\n## Stations\n\nA hairline ribbon of station names, duplicated, translating forever: Harbor, Saltworks, Low Bridge, Kiln, Glass City.\n\n## Chapters\n\nThree columns:\n\n1. Boarding — The platform is lit like a darkroom. You step on without a queue, and the door closes on a soft latch.\n2. The coast — Windows turn the water into a strip of sodium and black. Nobody narrates it. The glass does the work.\n3. Arrival — Glass City comes in as a low chord, not an announcement. The lights outside match the ones inside.\n\nFade them up with `motion` when they enter. Once only.\n\n## Close\n\nA border-top band. Serif line `The last carriage is still open.` Button `Reserve a window`.\n\n## Reduced motion and preview\n\nUnder `prefers-reduced-motion`, no scan, no ribbon movement, no fade offset. Everything is present.\n\nA `preview` prop renders only the opening card, filling its parent, so a gallery thumbnail stays a title card.\n\n## Done when\n\nThe page reads as one night journey from title to reservation, and reduced motion is a still editorial page.\n"
}
